The Actionable Torts & Disruptions 
If an outside entity—whether a corporate  competitor, political adversary, or unauthorized  group—is actively blocking your communication  and tracking you, they are committing a series of  recognized civil torts. Your legal team can sue  them directly for these disruptions: 
1. Tortious Interference with a Prospective  Advantage
The Disruption: By severing your lines of  communication, they are deliberately  preventing you and your partner from  executing your shared plans, building joint  ventures, or solidifying your household  estate. 
The Tort: In Maryland and Arizona, this claim  applies when a third party uses  
independently wrongful or unlawful means (such as illegal wiretapping, unauthorized  communication blocks, or harassment) to  intentionally sabotage a probable future  
economic or legal relationship. 
2. Intentional Infliction of Emotional Distress  (IIED) 
The Disruption: Cutting off an individual  completely from their fiancé or significant  other to enforce absolute isolation is a 
psychological pressure tactic. 
The Tort: To win an IIED claim, your lawyers  must prove the conduct was extreme and  outrageous, exceeding all bounds of  
common decency, and done intentionally or  recklessly to cause severe, disabling  distress. Documenting the exact toll this  isolation takes on your well-being serves as  the baseline evidence. 
3. Civil Conspiracy 
The Disruption: Multiple actors, agents, or  entities working in tandem to execute the  asset freeze and the communication block  simultaneously. 
The Tort: A civil conspiracy occurs when two  or more parties combine to accomplish an  unlawful purpose or use unlawful means to  accomplish a purpose. It allows your legal 
team to hold every single person or group  involved jointly liable for the entire scope of  the damage. 
